  <a href="#section-spring-paths" id="section-spring-paths" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose a Spring integration path</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>
		Spring can supply the same Simple Java Mail settings, so each profile can use its own SMTP server, credentials, transport strategy, and defaults.
      </p>
      <p>
		Spring integration lives in <code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code>. The Spring Boot starter adds that module. It declares Boot,
		Spring Framework, and SLF4J as provided dependencies, so the application gets their versions from its Boot parent or BOM.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable Spring integration paths">
        <table>
          <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Application path</th>
            <th>Dependency</th>
            <th>Activation</th>
          </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Spring Boot 2.7 or 3.x &mdash; recommended</td>
            <td><code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail-spring-boot-starter</code>, which brings in <code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code></td>
            <td>Automatic</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Spring Boot already using the module directly</td>
            <td><code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code></td>
            <td>Automatic</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Plain Spring</td>
            <td><code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code></td>
            <td><code class="inline language-java">@Import(SimpleJavaMailSpringSupport.class)</code></td>
          </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
		Every route exposes the same context-local <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code>,
		<code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code>, and default <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code> beans. There is no shared builder
		bean; inject the factory whenever you need a fresh builder.
      </p>
      <p>
		The <code class="inline language-properties">spring.mail.*</code> namespace and <code class="inline language-java">JavaMailSender</code> belong to Spring
		Mail, not this integration. Configure Simple Java Mail through <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.*</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="view">
      <p>
        All three routes load the conventional
        <code class="inline language-text">simplejavamail.properties</code> resource as a low-priority source, then resolve all matching values through
        Spring's <code class="inline language-java">Environment</code>. Spring remains responsible for profiles, placeholders, environment variables,
        system properties, and their precedence. Simple Java Mail does not read the raw process values again afterwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        Separate Spring application contexts keep separate snapshots and factories. Building or closing a Mailer in one context does not change the
        configuration used by another context in the same JVM.
      </p>
      <p>
        The context also starts when no SMTP settings are present. In that case the fallback host is <code class="inline language-text">localhost</code>;
        sending still requires an SMTP server there. Set <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.smtp.host</code> before sending anywhere
        without a local development SMTP service.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-versions" id="section-spring-versions" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Match the application's Spring Boot line</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>
		The same Simple Java Mail starter supports both Boot generations. Add it to an existing Boot application; the application's parent or BOM supplies
		the Boot, Spring Framework, and SLF4J versions.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable Spring Boot compatibility matrix">
        <table>
          <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Application line</th>
            <th>Application-supplied stack</th>
            <th>Compatibility checks</th>
          </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Spring Boot 2.7.x</td>
            <td>Spring Framework 5.3 and SLF4J 1.7</td>
            <td>Boot 2.7.18 on Java 11</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Spring Boot 3.x</td>
            <td>Spring Framework 6 and SLF4J 2</td>
            <td>Boot 3.0.13 on Java 17 and Boot 3.5.16 on Java 21</td>
          </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
		The checked versions are compatibility boundaries, not versions pulled into the application. Simple Java Mail keeps its own Java 11 bytecode
		baseline; the application must still meet the Java requirement of its chosen Boot line.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-installation" id="section-spring-installation" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Install and inject the Spring beans</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      In an application that already declares Spring Boot through its parent or BOM, add the Simple Java Mail starter with Maven:
      <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;simple-java-mail-spring-boot-starter&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;{{site.version}}&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
      Or with Gradle:
      <pre><code class="language-groovy">implementation("org.simplejavamail:simple-java-mail-spring-boot-starter:{{site.version}}")</code></pre>
	  In plain Spring, activate the same bean path explicitly:
	  <pre><code class="small">@Configuration
@Import(SimpleJavaMailSpringSupport.class)
class SimpleJavaMailConfiguration {
}</code></pre>
      Then inject the default Mailer directly; Boot needs no <code class="inline language-java">@Import</code>:
      <pre><code class="small">@Component
public class YourEmailService {

    private final Mailer mailer;

    public YourEmailService(Mailer mailer) {
        this.mailer = mailer;
    }
}
</code></pre>
      Inject the factory when the service builds emails or needs a fresh Mailer builder:
      <pre><code class="small">@Component
public class YourEmailService {

    private final SimpleJavaMail mail;

    public YourEmailService(SimpleJavaMail mail) {
        this.mail = mail;
    }

    public Email newStatusEmail() {
        return mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
                .withSubject("Status")
                .buildEmail();
    }
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-beans" id="section-spring-beans" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Customize Spring-managed beans</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      Replace the default <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code> with one built from a fresh builder:
      <pre><code class="small">@Configuration
public class MailConfiguration {

    @Bean(destroyMethod = "close")
    public Mailer customMailer(SimpleJavaMail mail) {
        return mail.mailerBuilder()
                .resetThreadPoolSize()
                .withThreadPoolKeepAliveTime(5000)
                .withProxyBridgePort(7777)
                .withExecutorService(new MyAwesomeCustomThreadPoolExecutor())
                .buildMailer();
    }
}
</code></pre>
      <p>
        Boot backs off independently for <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code>, <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code>,
        and <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code>. A replacement config feeds the two remaining defaults; a replacement factory feeds the default
        Mailer; and a replacement Mailer leaves the injectable config and factory available. Spring closes the Mailer it creates. Your replacement Mailer
        follows the destroy method declared or inferred by its own bean definition. Use <code class="inline language-java">@Bean(destroyMethod = "")</code>
        only when the application deliberately owns shutdown elsewhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        To disable all three defaults, use Spring Boot's standard exclusion:
      </p>
      <pre><code class="language-properties">spring.autoconfigure.exclude=org.simplejavamail.springsupport.SimpleJavaMailAutoConfiguration</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

	<a href="#section-spring-oauth2" id="section-spring-oauth2" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
	<h2>Provide OAuth2 access tokens through Spring</h2>

	<section>
		<div class="wide full-width-example">
			<p>
				When the default Mailer uses <code class="inline language-java">SMTP_OAUTH2</code>, a single
				<code class="inline language-java">OAuth2AccessTokenProvider</code> bean is picked up automatically:
			</p>
			<pre><code class="small">@Bean
OAuth2AccessTokenProvider smtpAccessTokens(TokenService tokenService) {
    return tokenService::currentAccessToken;
}</code></pre>
			<p>
				If the application has more than one <code class="inline language-java">OAuth2AccessTokenProvider</code> bean, mark the one for SMTP with
				<code class="inline language-java">@Primary</code> or use normal Spring bean disambiguation.
			</p>
			<p>
				Applications using Spring Security OAuth2 Client can adapt its
				<a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/client/core.html">OAuth2AuthorizedClientManager</a>. The manager obtains
				or refreshes the authorized client when needed; Simple Java Mail receives only the current token value.
			</p>
			<pre><code class="small">@Bean
OAuth2AccessTokenProvider smtpAccessTokens(
        OAuth2AuthorizedClientManager authorizedClients) {
    return () -&gt; {
        OAuth2AuthorizeRequest request = OAuth2AuthorizeRequest
                .withClientRegistrationId("smtp")
                .principal("simple-java-mail")
                .build();

        OAuth2AuthorizedClient client = authorizedClients.authorize(request);
        if (client == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Unable to authorize the SMTP OAuth2 client");
        }
        return client.getAccessToken().getTokenValue();
    };
}</code></pre>
			<p>
				For scheduled jobs and other code without an HTTP request, Spring Security recommends an
				<code class="inline language-java">AuthorizedClientServiceOAuth2AuthorizedClientManager</code>. This adapter stays in your application; the Simple
				Java Mail Spring module adds no Spring Security dependency.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<a href="#section-spring-profiles" id="section-spring-profiles" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
	<h2>Configure profiles and dynamic properties</h2>

  <section>

    <div class="wide">
      Spring profiles can provide different values, for example <code>default</code> and <code>production</code>:
      <pre><code class="language-properties small">#application.properties
simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug=true
simplejavamail.smtp.host=smtp.host
simplejavamail.smtp.port=25
simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTP
</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="language-properties small">#application-production.properties
simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug=false
simplejavamail.smtp.host=smtp.production.host
simplejavamail.smtp.port=465
simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTPS
simplejavamail.smtp.username=&lt;username&gt;
simplejavamail.smtp.password=&lt;password&gt;
simplejavamail.proxy.host=proxy.production.host
simplejavamail.proxy.port=1080
simplejavamail.proxy.username=&lt;proxy_username&gt;
simplejavamail.proxy.password=&lt;proxy_password&gt;
</code></pre>
      For per-cluster pool settings, Spring properties use the same dynamic namespace as regular property files:
      <pre><code class="language-properties small">#application.properties
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.clusterkey.uuid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.coresize=0
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.maxsize=3
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.claimtimeout.millis=30000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.expireafter.millis=600000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.loadbalancing.strategy=RANDOM_ACCESS

# or key the config directly by UUID
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.maxsize=8
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.loadbalancing.strategy=ROUND_ROBIN
</code></pre>
	</div>
  </section>
  <section>
	<div class="view">
	  <p>
		The Spring module includes configuration metadata for every supported <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.*</code> setting,
		giving compatible IDEs completion and type hints in <code class="inline language-text">application.properties</code> and YAML files. This metadata
		is editor assistance only: values are still resolved from Spring's <code class="inline language-java">Environment</code> when the application context
		starts. Dynamic namespaces such as <code class="inline language-properties">extraproperties.*</code> and connection-pool cluster names are recognized,
		but their application-defined child keys cannot be predicted. A few generated metadata aliases exist where Spring's relaxed binding cannot
		represent the original name cleanly.
	  </p>
	</div>

	<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug-out=SLF4J
simplejavamail.custom.sslfactory.clazz=org.mypackage.ssl.MySSLSocketFactoryClass
simplejavamail.defaults.poolsize-more.keepalivetime=2000
simplejavamail.smime.signing.keystore-password=keystore_password
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key-alias=key_alias
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key-password=key_password
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.private-key-file-or-data=file:my_dkim_key.der
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.signing-domain=your-domain.com
# Leave header exclusions unset unless a known relay rewrites a header; From must remain signed.
</code></pre>
	</div>
  </section>
